Now I can see the core semantic issue: `quantifiers.formal` concludes with `not exists q in I+ with gamma subset J^-(q) intersect M` — the whole-curve containment — while `visibility.definition` uses the TAIL formulation (`exists q AND t0 with gamma([t0,T)) subset J^-(q)`) and explicitly says whole-geodesic containment would misclassify. Also `D5` defines q by `gamma([0,T)) is contained in J^-(q)` (whole curve). And `visibility.negation_conclusion` says "for every q and every t0, the tail is NOT contained" — the correct negation of the tail predicate. So the formal quantifier's not_exists (whole curve) is NOT the negation of the class's canonical visibility predicate — it's *weaker* than (or different from) the intended statement. That's a real, decidable content defect (worker-19's critical HF).

Let me be careful: which direction? The class predicate P_WCC = "no visible singularity", where visible = exists q exists t0 tail ⊂ J^-(q). Negation: for all q for all t0, tail ⊄ J^-(q). The quantifiers.formal says `not exists q with gamma ⊆ J^-(q)` = for all q, whole curve ⊄ J^-(q). Since tail ⊆ whole curve, if whole curve ⊆ J^-(q) then tail ⊆ J^-(q). Contrapositive: if tail ⊄ J^-(q) then whole ⊄ J^-(q). So "for all q: whole ⊄ J^-(q)" is *implied by* "for all q, for all t0: tail ⊄ J^-(q)" but not conversely... Actually:

Tail-negation: ∀q∀t0 tail_{t0} ⊄ J⁻(q).
Whole-negation: ∀q whole ⊄ J⁻(q).

whole ⊄ J⁻(q) means ∃t: γ(t) ∉ J⁻(q). tail_{t0} ⊄ J⁻(q) means ∃t≥t0: γ(t) ∉ J⁻(q). The whole-negation is weaker per-q (easier): it could hold because of an early point outside J⁻(q) even though the tail is inside. So ∀q whole ⊄ J⁻(q) is *implied by* ∀q∀t0 tail ⊄ J⁻(q)? If tail ⊄ for every t0, then in particular for t0=0, whole ⊄. Yes: tail_{0} = whole curve, so ∀q∀t0 tail_{t0} ⊄ J⁻(q) ⟹ ∀q whole ⊄ J⁻(q). So the formal statement's consequent is implied by the intended one but is strictly weaker. Hence `quantifiers.formal` is weaker than `visibility.definition`/conclusion — exactly worker-19's claim: whole-curve vs tail contradiction; the operative formal statement misstates the class (a geodesic whose whole curve lies in J⁻(q) for some q but whose tail leaves J⁻(q) would be "visible" under the formal reading but not under the canonical tail predicate... wait let's check the other direction: formal says not exists q with whole ⊆ J⁻(q) — i.e., no q contains the whole curve. If a curve has whole ⊆ J⁻(q0) for some q0 then under the formal reading, the not-exists fails → the formal statement FAILS (i.e., the class conclusion is false for that data). Under the canonical tail reading, that curve is visible (tail = whole, t0=0). So same there. Consider a curve with an early excursion outside J⁻(q) for all q, but whose tail from t0 is inside J⁻(q0): canonical reading says it IS visible (tail), so a counterexample to the class; the formal reading says not-exists holds (no q contains the whole curve) → no counterexample. So the formal statement misses counterexamples → strictly weaker claim than the class. That's a real defect: the schema's formal quantifier does not state the class it claims.

D5 definition also uses whole curve `gamma([0,T))` — inconsistent with visibility.definition's tail. And `negation` prose says "some future-inextendible causal geodesic of finite affine length is visible from I+" — okay.

