```python
def finite_model_check():
    pts = (0,1,2)
    gammas = [g for n in (2,3) for g in itertools.permutations(pts,n)]
    past_choices = [frozenset(s) for s in all subsets of pts]
    qsets = [ (Jq,) for Jq in past_choices ] + [ (J1,J2) for J1,J2 in itertools.combinations_with_replacement(past_choices,2) ]
    impl_a = True  # canonical_fail -> formal_fail
    impl_b = True  # formal_fail -> canonical_fail
    witness = None
    for gamma in gammas:
        whole = set(gamma)
        for Q in qsets:
            visible = any(any(set(gamma[t0:]) <= set(Jq) for t0 in range(len(gamma))) for Jq in Q)
            canon_fail = not visible
            formal_fail = all(not (whole <= set(Jq)) for Jq in Q)
            if canon_fail and not formal_fail: impl_a=False; witness_a=...
            if formal_fail and not canon_fail: impl_b=False; witness_b=... (if None)
    ...
```

Note: canonical predicate "visible" = ∃q∈Q ∃t0: tail ⊆ J⁻(q). `canon_fail` = no visible singularity = the class conclusion. `formal_fail` = ∀q: whole ⊄ J⁻(q) = the consequent of quantifiers.formal (not exists q with whole ⊆). 

impl_a: canon_fail → formal_fail (does the class conclusion imply the formal statement?). impl_b: formal_fail → canon_fail (is the formal statement strong enough?). We expect impl_a True, impl_b False with witness where formal_fail true but canon_fail false (visible singularity exists but no q contains the whole curve).

